---
name: dev-sanity-task
version: 2.0.0
description: lead sanity check assignment for the member that fills the dev-sanity role (resolve-role dev-sanity). task_id is the sanity check bead. The member splits the checked bead per numbered deliverable (sanity-split), runs one check subagent per deliverable with lint alongside, merges (sanity-merge) and reports PASS or FAIL. Not QA.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - checked_bead
  - sprint
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - commit
  - base
  - pr_number
  - pr_url
  - primary_checkout
  - phase_root
optional_variables:
  - lead
  - cc
  - lint_command
defaults:
  lead: team-lead
  cc: team-lead
  lint_command: "just lint"
metadata:
  type: dev-sanity-task
  tags:
    - stage:dev-sanity
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-sanity-start
    stage: dev-sanity
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}" mode="dev-sanity">
  <description><![CDATA[Sanity check `{{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }}` at `{{ commit | string | cdata_escape }}`: nothing skipped, no obvious errors, lint passes. This is not QA.]]></description>

  <checked-bead><![CDATA[{{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }}]]></checked-bead>
  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <commit><![CDATA[{{ commit | string | cdata_escape }}]]></commit>
  <base><![CDATA[{{ base | string | cdata_escape }}]]></base>
  <pr number="{{ pr_number | string | cdata_escape }}"><![CDATA[{{ pr_url | string | cdata_escape }}]]></pr>
  <lint-command><![CDATA[{{ lint_command | string | cdata_escape }}]]></lint-command>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Set `run_started_at=$(date +%s)` before claim. Then run `test -n "{{ pr_number | string | cdata_escape }}" && test -n "{{ pr_url | string | cdata_escape }}"` or refuse `SANITY.PR_REQUIRED`. Then run `gh pr view {{ pr_number | string | cdata_escape }} --json baseRefName,headRefOid --jq '.baseRefName + " " + .headRefOid'`; require base `{{ base | string | cdata_escape }}` equal to `bd show {{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }} --json | jq -r '.[0].metadata | .stack_parent // .pr_target'` (else `SANITY.PR_TARGET_MISMATCH`) and head `{{ commit | string | cdata_escape }}` equal to its `metadata.stack_head` when set (else `SANITY.NOT_INTEGRATED`): the check runs only at the head rebased into the stack. Run `git fetch origin && git log --format=%H origin/{{ base | string | cdata_escape }}..{{ commit | string | cdata_escape }} | grep -q .` or refuse `SANITY.ZERO_DELTA`; run `test -z "$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no | grep -v -e '^?? \.beads\.gate\.lock$' -e ' \.sc-compose/')"` or refuse `SANITY.DIRTY_TREE` (the ignored paths are `IGNORED_STATUS_PATHS` in `scripts/assignment-gates.py`); run `bd history {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` and refuse `SANITY_FROZEN` when it records a prior PASS. Before any such refusal message or task close, strictly render `workflow-issue-bead.json.j2` with id `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-wf-<CODE>`, then `bd import <scratch>/{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-wf-<CODE>.json`, and state that id in the refusal. Otherwise claim and start the check.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `false` the bead is not ready: do not claim it and do not start the task. Find the root cause: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (normally `{{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }}` still open), and `bd show <blocker>` for each. Report it with `atm send {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} --stdin`: the bead, why it is not ready, and which bead or agent has to move. Then wait.]]></step>
    <step id="b"><![CDATA[Run the check as your agent prompt's Execution Steps say: `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/sanity-split --task {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }} --branch {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }} --commit {{ commit | string | cdata_escape }} --base {{ base | string | cdata_escape }} --lint-command '{{ lint_command | string | cdata_escape }}' --scratch <scratch>`, one check subagent per assignment it prints, then `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/sanity-merge <manifest> {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} {{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }} {{ sprint | string | cdata_escape }}` on the replies. The manifest's `sha` is `<sha>` below; the merge writes the vars for step e or e1. Route every non-zero exit of sanity-split or sanity-merge by your agent prompt's Error Handling; only when it says cannot run, go to step e2 with that code. `SANITY.PLAN_INVALID` means the bead has no numbered `## Deliverables` list, so also tell {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} that planning failed for `{{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }}`.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[PASS: close the bead and the task together: `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "PASS at <sha>"` and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/dev-sanity-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/sanity-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json` with `verdict` PASS. Closing the bead releases every dev bead that required `{{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }}`.]]></step>
    <step id="e1"><![CDATA[FAIL: the sanity bead must stay **open**, because closing it would release dependent dev steps. Preserve every finding unchanged. Before closing this task, run this exact command against the merge-created vars file; it creates one open `bug` per finding: a blocking finding is a child of `{{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }}` (so it holds the sprint open), and an important or minor finding is a child of the phase root `{{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }}` with the checked bead, sprint and commit in its metadata; copies the report fields verbatim to child metadata and description, and adds each declared inter-finding blocking edge. Do not reopen or otherwise edit the checked parent:

```bash
{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/sanity-create-findings \
  --task {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }} \
  --vars <scratch>/sanity-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json \
  --reviewer <sc-sanity-llm-or-sc-sanity-jev> \
  --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-number {{ pr_number | string | cdata_escape }} \
  --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY" > <scratch>/sanity-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-finding-children.json
```

`sanity-create-findings` creates finding children and only their declared inter-finding prerequisite edges. It never creates a finding-sanity gate or adds an edge to another planned sprint: `sprints.jsonl` is the sole authority for sprint-to-sprint dependencies.

The script gives each child the severity priority from `plan_contract` (blocking P1, important P2, minor P4) and copies the parent's required difficulty. The parent/child hierarchy itself is the parent closure gate; `bd` rejects a parent-to-child `blocks` edge because that would deadlock the child. For each report `depends_on` selector, the script creates `<dependent-finding-child> --blocks--> <prerequisite-finding-child>`, preventing the dependent fix from closing first. It adds the ordered `finding_bead_ids` array to the same vars file used by `atm task close`, so the final report links directly to every child; do not write those ids to parent notes because the graph is authoritative. Return the sanity bead to the queue with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --assignee "" --append-notes "FAIL at <sha>: <n> findings"`, then close the task with the same `atm task close … completed` command and `verdict` FAIL. A failure to create or wire every child is cannot-run, not a completed FAIL. {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} reviews the created child beads, then follows the existing process to reopen the parent and assign the dev fix; the lead may overrule, amend, split, or reassign children. The parent cannot close until all children close; only then may final parent closure occur, after which the next sanity run is ready.{% if cc and cc != lead %} Copy the verdict line to `{{ cc | string | cdata_escape }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc | string | cdata_escape }} --stdin`.{% endif %}]]></step>
    <step id="e2"><![CDATA[If the check cannot run (plan not splittable, worktree missing, commit not pushed, a check timed out or was rejected twice), first strictly render `workflow-issue-bead.json.j2` with id `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-wf-<CODE>`, then `bd import <scratch>/{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-wf-<CODE>.json`. Return the bead with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --assignee "" --append-notes "<code>; workflow issue {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-wf-<CODE>: <reason>"` and close the task with `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`.]]></step>
    <step id="e3"><![CDATA[Hard console-report completion gate for every completed PASS or FAIL run: after all required bead updates and after `atm task close` succeeds, append the run and render the newest ten rows. This is a user-visible terminal report, never an ATM message to the lead, and it is required before calling the run complete. Use the merge-created vars unchanged:

```bash
iteration=$(atm task events {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --all --json \
  | jq '[.events[] | select(.event == "completed")] | length')
{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/sanity-run-history \
  --vars <scratch>/sanity-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json \
  --task {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ checked_bead | string | cdata_escape }} \
  --commit {{ commit | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-number {{ pr_number | string | cdata_escape }} --iteration "$iteration" \
  --started-at "$run_started_at" --output <scratch>/sanity-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-table-vars.json --limit 10
sc-compose render --strict \
  --file {{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/sanity-run-table.md.j2 \
  --var-file <scratch>/sanity-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-table-vars.json
```

The render command itself must write the table to the terminal. Also include the complete rendered table as Markdown directly in your user-visible completion reply before reading ATM again; tool stdout, a file path, and the ATM task-close body do not satisfy this requirement. Preserve the exact `S | PR | Find | ✓ | Done | Iter` columns, order, and symbols without summarizing or redesigning the report. The ledger at `.sc/sanity-log/` is ignored runtime state and must never be committed. A refusal is not a completed run and does not append a ledger row. If append or render fails after the task close, print `SANITY.STATUS_TABLE_UNAVAILABLE` and the failure immediately in the user-visible completion reply; do not silently omit it.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[After the close and the required user-visible completion reply in step e3 (unless refused), read ATM again.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
